 adopted effectively  takes into account  all the genetic variation that will be  contributing to risk in an individual  but of course the time and the severity  of the symptoms in any one individual  will also be impacted by lifestyle and  environmental risk exposure  over the course of an individual's  lifetime  how has your polygenic risk score been  validated has there been any independent  verifications  so our polygenic risk or algorithm has  been validated in  multiple data sets um the cardiff  university  group on which our algorithm is based  has been validated in a number of  longitudinal  very well characterized samples  three or four independent verification  data sets  including individuals who  went on to die and then we were able to  look at post-mortem  histopathology confirmation in alzheimer  cases and age match controls  and that's a sort of gold standard  approach to validating  polygenic risk or algorithms and the in  fact the  the performance of the algorithm in that  particular study  was at something like 86 or 87 percent  which we think is at the theoretical  maximum for this type of  apologetic risk or approach  how do you design your axiom array to  make it as effective as possible for  polygenic risk or analysis  in ad  so we've designed the axiom array and  the we covariate  to contain the  ideal snip combination to run the  polygenic risk score  the genoscore analysis that as i  mentioned in my  presentation includes 114 000  snips or dna variants that are optimized  for the  cardiff algorithm or genoscore approach  and then we have a lot of other content  which allows us to do other forms of  analysis  including identifying individuals with  the  early onset high risk dna variants or  mutations  in protein in one prisonalin ii an  amyloid precursor protein  which we use as a quality control step  to make sure that  we're not dealing with an early onset  case  as we do our analyses we also  have dna variants associated with  other non-alzheimer disease dementias on  the array  again that allows us to broaden the type  of analysis we can do  in any one data set  who are the key potential customers for  your polygenic risk score  potential customers for our prs approach  today  include both academics and pharma  companies  pharma companies obviously are driven by  the need to  stratify the individuals at  highest risk into their clinical studies  so that they can optimize cohort  selection  for demonstrating therapeutic effects of  their particular  interventions be they pharmacological  new drugs  or other types of interventions so we've  been working with a number of those  companies over the last couple of years  also academics are interested in  understanding genetic and non-genetic  factors for alzheimer's disease risk and  so we work with  various academics to help them  understand  genetic risk profiles within their own  cohort their own longitudinal subject  cohorts  do you envision your array and software  going through a  510k regulatory approval  so our array and software which together  comprise  the ginator product are  we've been in discussion with the fda in  the us  for a few weeks we've decided  with their advice that we will pursue  initially  the approval and launch of a clear  ldt or laboratory developed test service  in the us with various lab partners  and in europe we will go down the ce  marked route and that allows us to get  this product  available to a very high quality  standard  by the end of this year for the u.s and  europe  and but beyond that we will be doing  the formal clinical validation studies  that would support a 510  510 k regulatory process and approval  uh in the us and and in europe  and we would imagine um envisage  starting those studies  later this year and then being the  position to to file for regulatory  approval  sometime during 2021  and that's the end of the live q a uh  for dr pipper's presentation i really  want to thank all of our speakers for  their time  uh and efforts today and for everyone  listening for the questions as well  this concludes the webinar sessions for  today but the environment will remain  live and open so please drop by our  booth and speak to us  and find out more about the solutions  that we have to help accelerate your  research  please also participate in a scavenger  hunt game look out for icons in the  environment to win some cool prizes  there will be a pop-up survey appearing  in a minute please  complete this to help us  evaluate this roadshow and thank you  again  for all of your attendance
 

